message.reasoning_content:
The user wants to add an ItemFilter feature to Scrapy feeds. The issue description proposes an ItemChecker class that will be used by FeedExporter to decide if an item should be allowed to be exported to a particular feed.

From the code we saw in feedexport.py, the FeedExporter class has an item_scraped method that processes each item and exports it to all slots (feeds). We need to modify this to check if the item passes the filter for each feed before exporting.

We need to look at how feed_options are stored and used. In the FeedExporter.__init__, self.feeds is a dict mapping uri to feed_options (which includes format, item_filter, etc.). In item_scraped, we iterate over self.slots (each slot corresponds to a feed). Each slot has feed_options accessible via self.feeds[slot.uri_template] (as seen in the batch creation part). Actually, slot has uri_template attribute.

So we need to check if slot.feed_options has an 'item_filter' key, and if so, instantiate or use it to check the item.

But the proposal says: ItemChecker class can be declared in settings.py. For convenience Items can also be declared here without needing to create a custom ItemChecker class.

They show:
FEEDS = {
    'items1.json': {
        'format': 'json',
        'item_filter': MyFilter1,
    },
    'items2.xml': {
        'format': 'xml',
        'item_classes': (MyItem1, MyItem2),
    },
}

So feed_options may have either 'item_filter' (a class) or 'item_classes' (a tuple/list of classes). The ItemChecker class should accept feed_options in __init__ and populate accepted_items from item_classes if present.

We need to implement the ItemChecker class somewhere, likely in scrapy/utils or scrapy/extensions. Then modify FeedExporter.item_scraped to use it.

Let's first see if there's any existing filter-related code. Search for "item_filter" in the codebase.

